**Key design decisions:**

- **`copy_path`** opens the source (following symlinks via `File::open`), reads source permissions, then opens the destination with `O_WRONLY|O_CREAT|O_TRUNC`. The `mode()` on `OpenOptions` only takes effect at creation time (kernel applies `mode & ~umask`), so existing files retain their permissions automatically. Dangling symlinks, directories, and missing parents all produce `open()` errors naturally.

- **Self-copy detection** uses `fs::canonicalize` on both source and effective destination, comparing resolved paths. This catches filesystem aliases (bind mounts, symlinks, `./` prefixes).

- **`OsString` throughout** ensures non-UTF-8 pathname bytes are preserved (Linux `OsString` is a byte buffer). `os_display` uses `to_string_lossy` only for diagnostic messages.

- **No panics**: all I/O uses `?` or explicit `match`; no `unwrap`/`expect` on fallible operations.

- **Interpretation chosen**: "Options may appear anywhere before `--`" with zero implemented options in this checkpoint means any dash-prefixed token (except bare `-`) before `--` is an unknown-option error. After `--`, all tokens are literal operands regardless of leading dashes.
